 "input_sha256": "86aaf7ca41be60140f11dd4fed84bffed2590746dcb267d4e9947128974336be",
 "explanation": "Two hooks in the stock SysEx path (the command dispatcher and the out-of-range branch of the command executor) give the commands 0x46-0x4E fixed argument counts and handlers through a table of nine words (count and handler offset per command; erased = no such command: every later indexed command writes only its own entry). Request F0 01 28 46 bb pp F7 (beat 0-15, pad 0-31) answers F0 01 28 47 bb pp <152 bytes> F7 on the SysEx cable set in System: the 152 bytes are byte for byte the body of the 60 message the machine sends for that Sound (request 40 when that slot is the selection). The machine's Sound serializer writes parameter 124 = 3 into the Sound it reads; the request reads that parameter first and writes it back when it was not 3, so it leaves the slot as it found it (the reply carries 3, as request 40). Out of range (beat 16 is the Revert copy): F0 01 28 4F 46 01 F7, the status message (command, code: 0 done, 1 index out of range). Every other command takes the stock path unchanged. No RAM. Proof: tools/sim_indexed_sysex.py. Docs/analyses/study-indexed-sysex.md.",
 "commands": {
  "F0 01 28 46 bb pp F7": "Sound of beat bb (0-15), pad pp (0-31); reply F0 01 28 47 bb pp <152 bytes = the 60 body> F7, or F0 01 28 4F 46 01 F7 when out of range"
 },
